(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-skin-hair-nail-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Skin, Hair & Nail Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The skin is described as the heaviest single organ, making up approximately what percentage of total body weight?","choices":["50%","90%","1%","16%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The skin is the heaviest single organ in the body, making up approximately 16% of total body weight. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The skin's listed functions include retaining body fluids, protecting underlying tissues, modulating temperature, and...","choices":["producing insulin","synthesizing vitamin D","filtering blood","producing red blood cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Among its key functions, the skin synthesizes vitamin D (via UV-light-driven synthesis), in addition to retaining body fluids, protecting underlying tissue, and modulating temperature. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The three layers of the skin, from most superficial to deepest, are the epidermis, dermis, and...","choices":["muscularis mucosae layer","the periosteum layer","subcutaneous tissue","the visceral pleura"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three skin layers, from superficial to deep, are the epidermis (containing the stratum corneum, stratum basale, and stratum spinosum), the dermis (containing glands, hair follicles, and nerve terminals), and the subcutaneous tissue. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which structures are located within the dermis layer of the skin?","choices":["Only red and yellow bone marrow tissue, with nothing else present","Only the stratum corneum, which is actually part of the epidermis instead","Sebaceous glands, sweat glands, hair follicles, and cutaneous nerve terminals","Nothing at all; the dermis is a completely inert, structureless layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dermis (the middle layer, deep to the epidermis) contains sebaceous glands, sweat glands, hair follicles, and cutaneous nerve terminals. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Skin color variation depends on several pigments, including melanin (a brownish pigment) and hemoglobin. Oxyhemoglobin, found in arteries/capillaries, produces a...","choices":["darker bluish color","yellow color","bright red color","no color change at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Oxyhemoglobin, the oxygenated form of hemoglobin found in arteries and capillaries, produces a bright red pigment, in contrast to deoxyhemoglobin (found in veins), which has a darker, bluish color. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Deoxyhemoglobin, found in veins, contributes a darker pigment with what color quality compared with oxyhemoglobin?","choices":["a bright red color","an identical color to oxyhemoglobin","a bluish color","a bright yellow color"],"answer":2,"exp":"Deoxyhemoglobin (the deoxygenated form found in veins) has a darker pigment with a bluish quality, in contrast to the bright red color of oxyhemoglobin in arteries/capillaries. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Carotene, one of the pigments affecting skin color, is a yellow pigment found in...","choices":["only within circulating red blood cells","found only within the hair follicle shaft","only within the epidermis' deepest stratum basale layer","subcutaneous fat and heavily keratinized areas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carotene is a yellow pigment found in subcutaneous fat and heavily keratinized areas of skin (such as the palms and soles). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Bilirubin, a yellow-brown pigment that can affect skin color, is released into circulation from...","choices":["prolonged sun exposure exclusively, with no other source","direct secretion from active sebaceous glands","the ongoing synthesis of new melanin pigment","the breakdown of heme molecules from red blood cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bilirubin is a yellow-brown pigment released into the circulation as a breakdown product of heme (from red blood cell turnover); elevated levels can cause jaundice, a yellowing of the skin. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Sebaceous glands, which secrete a fatty substance onto the skin via hair follicles, are present on all skin surfaces except...","choices":["the palms and soles","the entire body, with no exceptions","the face","the scalp"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sebaceous glands are present on essentially all skin surfaces except the palms and soles, which lack hair follicles (through which sebaceous glands normally secrete). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Apocrine sweat glands, distinct from the more widely distributed eccrine glands, are primarily located in the...","choices":["axillary and groin regions, opening primarily into hair follicles","everywhere on the body, identical in distribution to eccrine glands","the palms and soles exclusively, opening directly onto skin","the scalp exclusively, opening directly onto the skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Apocrine glands are primarily located in the axillary and groin regions and open primarily into hair follicles, in contrast to eccrine glands, which are widely distributed and open directly onto the skin surface. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Vellus hair, one of the two hair types, is best described as...","choices":["found exclusively on the scalp and eyebrows","coarse and heavily pigmented, like scalp hair","short, fine, and less pigmented ('peach fuzz')","entirely absent in all healthy individuals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vellus hair is short, fine, and lightly pigmented — informally called 'peach fuzz' — in contrast to terminal hair, which is coarser and more heavily pigmented (for example, scalp hair, eyebrows). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The lunula, a nail structure, is best described as...","choices":["the tissue protecting the sides of the nail plate","the white, half-moon shape at the proximal nail bed","the tissue protecting the space between the proximal nail fold and nail plate","the colored portion of the nail plate reflecting the vasculature of the nail bed"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lunula is the whitish, half-moon-shaped area visible at the proximal end of the nail bed, a normal nail structure. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"The nail plate normally gets its color from the vasculature of the nail bed and should appear as a variation of...","choices":["pink","pure white with no variation","bright yellow","deep blue at baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"The nail plate's color derives from the underlying nail bed vasculature and should normally appear as some variation of pink, though shape and opacity can vary, and pigment bands may be a normal finding in darker-skinned individuals. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"When taking the skin/hair/nail history, common or concerning symptoms to screen for include hair loss, rashes, growths, sores, lumps/bumps, dryness, itching, and...","choices":["increased urinary frequency, especially at night","new-onset chest pain during exertion","changes (for example, in moles or existing lesions)","new-onset intermittent visual disturbances"],"answer":2,"exp":"The symptom checklist includes hair loss, rashes, growths, sores, lumps/bumps, dryness, itching, and any changes noted by the patient, such as changes in existing moles or lesions. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"When screening for concerning mole changes in the history, the examiner specifically asks about changes in...","choices":["nothing; mole changes are not screened for","only the mole's exact anatomic location, never other features","size, shape, color, or sensation","only the patient's age"],"answer":2,"exp":"The history specifically screens for moles that have changed in size, shape, color, or sensation, key features that can raise concern for malignant transformation. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"The recommended approach to opening the skin/hair/nail history is to...","choices":["only ask about a single narrow topic, such as current medications, right away first","start with a closed yes/no question asking about one specific suspected diagnosis first","skip the history-taking step entirely and proceed directly to the physical exam itself","start with an open-ended question, such as 'tell me what has been going on with your skin/hair/nails'"],"answer":3,"exp":"The recommended approach begins with an open-ended question or statement, allowing the patient to describe their concerns in their own words before the examiner asks more specific follow-up questions. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Past medical history relevant to the skin/hair/nail exam includes chronic illness with skin manifestations, surgical procedures, immunosuppression, pregnancies, psychiatric disease, and...","choices":["only a history of dental and orthodontic procedures","a history of blistering sunburns or radiation exposure","nothing beyond a history of chronic illness alone","only a history of prior visual acuity screening tests"],"answer":1,"exp":"The past medical history checklist includes a history of blistering sunburns and radiation exposure, both relevant risk factors for skin conditions including skin cancer. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Family history relevant to the skin/hair/nail exam includes skin cancers, atopic conditions (eczema, asthma), psoriasis, hair loss, and...","choices":["only kidney disease","cardiac valve disease exclusively","pigmentation patterns","nothing; family history is not relevant here"],"answer":2,"exp":"The family history checklist includes skin cancers, atopic conditions (eczema, asthma, allergies), psoriasis, hair loss, and pigmentation patterns. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"The four general physical examination techniques applied to the skin exam are inspection, palpation, percussion, and auscultation, though for skin the most heavily used are...","choices":["percussion and auscultation exclusively","none of the four techniques apply to skin","only auscultation","inspection and palpation"],"answer":3,"exp":"While all four general exam techniques are mentioned, the skin exam relies most heavily on inspection ('what do you see?') and palpation ('what do you feel?'), since percussion and auscultation have limited direct application to skin assessment itself. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"The 'integrated examination' approach to the skin, as distinguished from a dedicated full-body exam, involves...","choices":["never examining the skin at all, regardless of what other components of the physical exam are being performed","examining only the patient's face, regardless of what other exam components are simultaneously being performed","a completely separate, dedicated full-body exam performed only once, during the patient's initial intake visit","inspecting/palpating exposed skin that becomes accessible during other exam components (for example, back/chest during lung auscultation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The integrated examination approach assesses skin opportunistically as it becomes exposed during other parts of the physical exam — for example, the back and chest during lung auscultation, or the abdomen during the abdominal exam — rather than as one dedicated, separate full-body skin exam. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"On skin inspection, the examiner assesses color, uniformity, thickness, symmetry, hygiene, lesions, scars, rashes, dryness, and...","choices":["bowel sounds","heart rate","odors","blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin inspection checklist includes color, uniformity, thickness, symmetry, hygiene, lesions, scars, rashes, dryness, and odors. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"On skin palpation, the examiner assesses moisture, texture, and temperature; temperature is best assessed using...","choices":["a stethoscope","visual inspection alone, without touching the patient","the palmar surface of the hands exclusively","the dorsal surface of the examiner's hands"],"answer":3,"exp":"Skin temperature is best assessed using the dorsal (back) surface of the examiner's hands, which is more sensitive to temperature differences, checking for bilateral symmetry. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Skin turgor, assessed on palpation, refers to...","choices":["the overall color pattern and pigmentation seen across the skin surface","the overall presence and distribution of any visible skin lesions found","the overall pattern and density of body hair distribution observed","the fullness or tension produced by the fluid content of cells and tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Turgor reflects the fullness/tension of the skin from its fluid content; normal skin should feel resilient, move easily when gently pinched, and return to place immediately when released. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Normal skin mobility, assessed alongside turgor, means the skin should...","choices":["remain tented/raised for an extended period after being pinched, unlike normal mobile skin","move easily when pinched and return to place immediately when released","only ever be assessed visually, and never through direct touch","remain completely rigid and immobile whenever it is gently pinched"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal skin should be resilient and mobile — moving easily when gently pinched and snapping back to its original position immediately upon release; delayed return (tenting) can suggest dehydration or decreased turgor. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"On nail inspection, the examiner evaluates pigmentation, length, symmetry, cleanliness, condition of the free edge/cuticle, and...","choices":["the patient's resting blood pressure","bilateral lung sounds on exam","active bowel sounds present","ridging, beading, pitting, or peeling"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nail inspection checklist includes ridging, beading, pitting, and peeling, along with pigmentation, length, symmetry, and condition of the free edge and cuticle. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When palpating the nail plate, the examiner assesses texture, firmness, thickness, uniformity, and...","choices":["resting blood pressure reading","adherence to the nail bed","chest wall expansion pattern","bowel sound character and pitch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nail plate palpation specifically assesses texture, firmness, thickness, uniformity, and adherence to the underlying nail bed, checking for any abnormal separation (onycholysis). — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"The normal nail base angle, formed by the proximal nail fold and nail plate, should measure approximately...","choices":["360 degrees","30 degrees","160 degrees","0 degrees (flat, with no angle)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The normal nail base angle (Lovibond angle), formed between the proximal nail fold and the nail plate, should measure approximately 160 degrees; an increased angle (approaching or exceeding 180 degrees) can be a sign of clubbing. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"When inspecting and palpating the proximal and lateral nail folds, the examiner screens for redness, swelling, pain, exudate, and...","choices":["active bowel sounds present","warts, cysts, or tumors","new-onset heart murmurs","elevated blood pressure reading"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nail fold assessment specifically screens for redness, swelling, pain, exudate, and growths such as warts, cysts, or tumors. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Equipment useful for the skin exam includes a light source, ruler, and optionally a magnifying lens or...","choices":["an otoscope","a stethoscope exclusively","no equipment is ever needed","dermoscope/dermatoscope"],"answer":3,"exp":"A dermoscope (or dermatoscope), which provides magnification with cross-polarized or unpolarized light, is an optional but increasingly common piece of equipment for the skin exam, alongside a light source, ruler, and magnifying lens. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Regarding glove use during the skin exam, the general practice is that gloves are...","choices":["only ever used specifically during hair and scalp inspection","never used under any circumstance during the entire skin exam","required for every single skin exam performed, without any exception","generally not used except for palpation of wounds/open lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gloves are generally not required for a routine skin exam but should be used when palpating wounds or open lesions, consistent with standard precautions for potential body fluid contact. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 24"}]};
